# a comparison narrows its operand on the edges behind its Branch, and the narrower name stands for the operand in all that the block on the edge dominates.
# A hundred values, each compared once, the comparisons in one another: the list of the innermost block has a hundred names. In a list that long a name is found by its value,
# and is then asked whether the list of the block holds it: each value has two names, one of the edge that was taken and one of the arm beside it, and only the first holds further in.
# function steps(n) { if (n is an int32) { const a1 = n & 1001, a2 = n & 1002, ..; if (1 < a1) { if (2 < a2) { .. return a100 + 1; } return a1 + 1; } return a1 + 1; } return n; }
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=none+TypedOperations+UnboxedNumbers
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# the arms beside the way in: the first knows a1 <= 1, each other takes the name that the comparison before it has left
# CHECK: int[1, 2] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[3, 1002] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[35, 1034] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[66, 1065] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[100, 1099] @Int32 = Increment
# the innermost block: a1, a2, a33, a64, a99, a100
# CHECK: int[3, 1002] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[4, 1003] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[35, 1034] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[66, 1065] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[101, 1100] @Int32 = Increment
# CHECK: int[102, 1101] @Int32 = Increment
# the last arm
# CHECK: int[101, 1100] @Int32 = Increment

module m0 path="main.js" displayPath="main.js" body=f0
